00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.070 align:start position:0%
 
joined i by the shadow of foreign  secretary david lammy hello to mr lambie  thank you for joining us on the  programme um  he was working really hard  uh it was the middle of a global  pandemic he took 10 minutes out what's  wrong with that  okay  the rules are clear that you couldn't  have a party  of more than two people a social  gathering of more than two people  indoors  what we know is that 30 people gathered  that his wife was there  and that the interior designer was there  as well  um if it was my birthday  and my wife  and an interior designer rocked up at  work  uh there was christmas cake birthday  cake and a celebration  it breaches the rules and the serious  side of this is that  at this time  the queen wasn't having a birthday party  boris had tweeted  just a few weeks earlier  a young seven-year-old girl who didn't  have her birthday party i remember my  own children not having birthday parties  we made a big cake for them we'd tweet  out and then people would say happy  birthday because they knew they couldn't  have a party  why was there one rule  for young people across the country who  couldn't have birthday parties for our  own queen and sovereign who couldn't  have a birthday party but a different  situation for boris johnson it's the  same drip drip drip and it's happening  at a time when people are dealing with a  cost of living crisis  energy prices  inflation  and we've got this serious problem in  ukraine what is going on why is he still  in office this was earlier on in the day  he was given a cake at a school but when  he went back to dining street it was the  cabinet room so it was a place of work  and the chancellor was there as well so  it it was a work meeting  look  the semantics of this is just so it's  unbearable  it's unbearable  people were putting up with sacrifices  number 10 should have been all about  work  in fact  um  you know most of westminster was very  quiet because many people were working  from home there were no other parties in  the whole of the palace of westminster  there weren't parties here  in sky or any of the other television  broadcasters it just wasn't done people  were taking the rules very very  seriously indeed except in number 10  downing street  that's what happened and i've got to  tell you we have a tradition in our  country  um  that  an englishman's word is his bond  we've had former prime ministers leave  office when they've got it wrong  chamberlain went left office when he  couldn't  unite the country with the unity  government winston churchill left office  when he felt he was too old and frail  theresa may left office when  she  messed up the general election david  cameron left office because he messed up  the referendum and we still have boris  johnson in office despite  imposing very tough restrictions on most  of the country  laws that he put in place that we all  had to live by  and yet we hear week after week after  week in these very serious times that  there were breaches in his number 10 and  he doesn't have to meet those rules it's  totally unacceptable either an  englishman's word is worth his bond or  not but he was i think he was born in  malta actually wasn't he or somewhere  anyway by the way um  he he set up sugray uh and the  investigation surely we have to wait for  that look he should have gone weeks ago  it frankly what is sue gray  investigating it's it's clear that there  were breaches of the rule let's let's go  back on this as well sue  okay first off first off he said first i  would have quite enjoyed investigation  first off he said  there were no parties  um  um there was no breach of the rules  then he said oh i'm furious that there  were parties um and um  allegra stratton  resigned  sean bailey i think  the conservative contender he also  resigned from his role in the greater  london assembly  then he moved on to  um i'm sorry that there were parties and  i apologize  um  but i didn't realize that i was walking  into a party uh and no one told me what  the rules were rules that he  made  this just goes on and on it's unbearable  it's unbearable who was in the cabinet  room um face a fixed penalty notice  what i can tell you is that at that time  i had constituents  who breached those very same rules  and they were subject  to uh criminal investigation and  sometimes criminal prosecution i had a  business shut down because it breached  the rules i had parties shut down by the  police because they breached the rules  and young people brought through the  courts  again why are there rules for my  constituents but not for boris johnson  is that a yes absolutely does that  include the chancellor look  we cannot have a country  in which any of us and that includes me  is above the law  but in this circumstance the prime  minister should simply resign step aside  and go and let someone else get on with  the job of running this very serious  country at this very very serious time  did he mislead parliament  he clearly misled  parliament he clearly missed that  parliament  it looks like on more than one occasion  and what do the rules say if you mislead  parliament you should go  it's a very serious business  to mislead your colleagues  and  even if it's unintentional  uh as i say at first he said that there  were no parties and there are there were  parties  so that was misleading parliament then  he said he didn't attend any parties and  it's clear that he did attend parties so  it's clear that he misled parliament uh  we don't have much time left but i do  want to ask you what's happening as far  as ukraine is concerned i did put it to  the transport secretary and he's  basically said watching brief at the  moment  we've heard from the united states that  they've got eight and a half thousand  troops on standby what should we be  doing either as uk troops or in the  guise of nato  well when i was in kiev a few weeks ago  what was clear is that they want the  full diplomatic support of the uk and  economic support and we stand with them  with that support we've got  800 troops in estonia at the moment  and of course the ukrainians  were talking about arms and how they can  support themselves in the event of an  invasion it was right the eu yesterday  provide them with economic support  and there may be more that we can do in  that regard in the coming days these are  very very serious times it's hugely  important that we stand  with our ukrainian allies  and very important for those  countries within nato that border  ukraine and russia that are very very  worried because what this represents is  a sort of expansionist  poutine sure but doesn't reflect the  sovereignty of countries around him what  do you think of the rhetoric though from  the ukrainian ambassador to the uk who  was sat just there a couple of weeks ago  talking about being on the edge of the  third world war i mean that that sort of  rhetoric doesn't help  look i i think that we have seen  expansionist ideals in europe before  um  and it's important to understand that  at the collapse of the soviet union  and the berlin wall that  ukraine gave up its nuclear weapons  and it gave up those nuclear weapons in  return for its independence its  sovereignty and the support of its  neighbours  and vladimir putin is breaching that  with that incursion  on the eastern side of ukraine  the annexing of crimea and now with  amassing troops along the borders of the  country he is breaching that and it's  important that the  western allies respond to that with  strength and are absolutely clear there  will be severe consequences but words  have consequences and we need to be  careful with the rhetoric  we want to dial down the rhetoric we  want the escalation  but in the end  dialogue must continue that  de-escalation must be  puting stepping back from what appears  to be  an escalation of troops because it is  his intention to invade that country or  else what  well  we've said that there would be very  severe sanctions  sanctions would damage the russians  economic sanctions would damage the  russian economy greatly  growth in russia last year was 0.3  percent it was 2.3 globally so  russia has already been hurt by  sanctions this would go much further  cutting russia out of the swift economic  system  uh  causing the real problem  where you can move the bankruptcy where  you can move money around  their currency the ruble has already  fallen uh overnight it will fall further  um and there are things that they  require like semiconductors that could  be we could impose sanctions as well so  uh all of that would cause damage to  them but yes gas that we have and our  fuel bills are ready astronomical  absolutely no one no one doubts that  sanctions don't hurt those imposing them  as well but you impose them because you  believe that it's a price worth paying  and it must be the case that the  democracy and sovereignty of the  ukrainians  is worth us giving them that support mr  lambie we must leave it there thank you  so much for joining us on the programme  this morning thank you

